---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * Sibling of fitnotes-alternative.astro. Read that file's header first; the
 * trademark rules and the source-checking rule are identical.
 *
 * NO CLAIMS ABOUT THEIR PRICING OR THEIR FEATURE SET. Competitor pricing moves,
 * and a page that is wrong about it is worse than a page that never mentioned
 * it. Everything comparative here is a statement about what BADGER does not
 * charge for, which we can keep true.
 *
 * Import claims checked against strong_parser.dart and strong_importer.dart on
 * 2026-09-05:
 *   - delimiter sniffed from the header line, ';' or ',', for the European
 *     exports (strong_parser.dart:61)
 *   - weight unit read from the 'Weight (kg)' / 'Weight (lbs)' header, not
 *     guessed (:91)
 *   - carried: Set Order, Reps, Distance, Seconds, Notes (per set), Workout
 *     Notes, Workout Name, RPE (:96-107, importer :242-243)
 *   - session start/end resolved from Strong's full datetime Date column via
 *     resolveSessionTimes (:134)
 *   - NOT carried: warmups. Strong's export does not mark them, and nothing in
 *     the parser infers them from Set Order. Do not add a warmup claim here.
 */

const description =
  'Move to Badger from Strong without losing your log. What the CSV export carries across, including your RPE and per-set notes, and what none of it costs.';
---

<HelpLayout title="Moving from Strong" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="Moving to Badger from Strong"
    description={description}
    dateModified="2026-09-24"
    about={['Strong', 'Workout tracking']}
    breadcrumb={[
      { name: 'Guides', url: '/guides/' },
      { name: 'Switching to Badger', url: '/guides/switching/' },
    ]}
  />

  <p class="crumb"><a href="/guides/switching/">Switching to Badger</a></p>

  <h1>Moving to Badger from Strong</h1>

  <p>
    If you would rather not rent your training log, or want it on your phone instead of in an
    account, your Strong history can come with you. This page covers what the export brings across
    and what you trade.
  </p>

  <h2 id="import">What the CSV carries across</h2>

  <p>Strong exports a CSV, and Badger keeps more of it than your sets.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Every set</strong>, with its date, exercise, weight, reps, distance, and time.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your RPE</strong>, on each set, so years of "how hard was that" are still there.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Per-set notes, on their set.</strong> The note on one heavy triple stays with that
      triple. Workout notes and the workout's name become notes on the session.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Session times.</strong> Imported workouts show when they started and how long they ran.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kilograms or pounds</strong>, read from the file's header and kept in the unit you
      logged, with no rounding.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The semicolon-separated export</strong> Strong writes in some European locales. There is
      nothing to convert.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your PRs</strong>, worked out from the imported history, so your records and graphs
      start out full.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What does not come across:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Warmups, as warmups.</strong> Strong's export does not mark them, so every set arrives
      as a working set.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your routines.</strong> The export holds history only. Start from one of Badger's ten
      built-in programs or build your own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your exercise categories.</strong> New exercises land in an "Imported" category for you
      to sort. An exercise already in your Badger library is matched by name.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how">Doing it</h2>

  <p>
    In Strong, go to <strong>Profile &rarr; Settings &rarr; Export Data</strong> to get a
    <code>strong.csv</code>, move it to the phone you train with, then open <strong>Settings &rarr;
    Your data &rarr; Import from Strong</strong> in Badger. Import each file once. The full steps are
    in <a href="/help/getting-started/#importing">importing from another app</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-you-get">What you get, and what you give up</h2>

  <p><strong>What you get:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No account.</strong> Your log is on your phone, with nothing to sign in to or be locked
      out of.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No paywall.</strong> No cap on routines, history or graphs, and exporting is free.
      <a href="/guides/workout-tracker-no-subscription/">The no-subscription guide</a> compares the
      trackers people usually weigh up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gym and machine tracking</strong>, with PRs kept per machine.
      <a href="/help/gyms/">How it works</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A plate calculator and warmup generator</strong> that work from the plates your gym
      owns, and <a href="/help/progress/">graphs</a> with estimated 1RM, muscle balance and plateau
      detection.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What you give up:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Sync between devices.</strong> Your log lives on one phone. Moving to a new one means
      carrying a backup file over: <a href="/help/phone-migration/">moving to a new phone</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Social features.</strong> There is nobody to follow and nothing to post.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="getting-out">Getting back out again</h2>

  <p>
    Your numbers stay yours if you move on. Every set exports to CSV, and the whole database backs up
    to a file you keep. Routines, exercises and equipment share as <code>.badger</code> files, a
    <a href="/badger-format/">documented format</a> you can read in any text editor.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    Strong is a trademark of its owner, and Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by,
    or connected to it in any way. This page describes compatibility only: Badger reads the CSV file
    that Strong exports.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  .crumb {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
